  # unit tests for upload_template
  # Create a template from an uploaded PDF or Word document
  # Creates a PDF template from a PDF, or a Word template from a Word document (.docx) or an OpenDocument text file (.odt), sent with the request (`multipart/form-data`). A Word template needs its `output_format`. For large files, upload the file with the presigned upload (GET /uploads/presign) and create the template with POST /templates. 
  # @param document A PDF (for a PDF template) or a Word document (.docx or .odt, for a Word template). DocSpring converts an OpenDocument text file (.odt) to a Word document after responding: the template's `document.state` is `converting` until then (sync.api hosts wait for it).
  # @param [Hash] opts the optional parameters
  # @option opts [Boolean] :wait Wait until the PDF has been processed, a `document_url`'s PDF or Word document downloaded, and an OpenDocument file converted, before responding. Default true on sync.api hosts.
  # @option opts [String] :name The template's name.
  # @option opts [String] :description The template's description.
  # @option opts [String] :parent_folder_id The folder to put the template in. Left out, it's created at the root.
  # @option opts [AiFieldNamingLanguage] :ai_field_naming_language The language AI field setup names fields in: `auto` uses the form's language when it's written in the Latin alphabet without accents, and English otherwise; `english` always uses English; `form_language` uses the form's language and script. Default: your account's template settings.
  # @option opts [Boolean] :detect_fields Set up the PDF's fields as DocSpring's "Automatically detect fields" upload option does: import or detect the fields, name them with AI, and set up conditional logic. `field_source`, `field_naming` and `field_logic` choose how. Default false. `document.page_processing_statuses` and `document.field_logic_status` show how it's going, and `document.state` becomes `processed` once every page's fields are set up.
  # @option opts [FieldSetupSource] :field_source With `detect_fields`, where the fields come from. `auto` (the default) imports the form fields of a fillable PDF and detects the fields of a PDF without them; `pdf_detection` detects the fields from the page layout, even on a fillable PDF; `pdf_form_fields` only imports the PDF's form fields.
  # @option opts [FieldSetupNaming] :field_naming With `detect_fields`, how the fields are named. `ai` (the default) names them and sets their types and settings with AI; `preserve` keeps the names of the PDF's form fields, and AI only names the fields it detected. `preserve` needs `field_source` `auto` or `pdf_form_fields`.
  # @option opts [FieldSetupLogic] :field_logic With `detect_fields`, whether AI reads the whole form once the fields are named and sets up conditional logic and required fields (`ai`) or not (`none`). Default: your account's template settings.
  # @option opts [OutputFormat] :output_format What each submission of a Word template makes unless its request sets `output_formats`: `docx` (a Word document), `odt` (an OpenDocument file, converted from the Word document) or `pdf`. Required when `document` is a Word document.
  # @option opts [Boolean] :include_office_use_fields With `detect_fields`, whether AI field setup keeps the fields that only the office receiving the form fills in, such as a "For office use only" box (`true`), or leaves them out (`false`). Default: your account's template settings.
  # @option opts [String] :idempotency_key A key of your own, such as a UUID, that makes it safe to send this request again: for 24 hours, DocSpring answers a request with the same key from the same API token with the first request's response (and an `Idempotent-Replayed: true` header) instead of processing it again. Send a new key with each new request, and the same key each time you send the same request again, such as after a timeout, a network error or a 5xx. The same key with a different request is a 422 `idempotency_key_reused`. A 409 `idempotency_key_in_use` with a `Retry-After` header means the first request is still being processed: send the request again then. A repeated response is the first one as it was, so its `download_url` links may have expired: use `permanent_url`, or get the record again for new links.
  # @return [Template]
